<Project Sdk="Microsoft.VisualStudio.JavaScript.Sdk/1.0.5277448">
  <PropertyGroup>
    <!--
      Fixes: "Your project does not reference '.NETCoreApp,Version=v6.0' framework. Add a
      reference to '.NETCoreApp,Version=v6.0' in the 'TargetFrameworks' property ... and then
      re-run NuGet restore." (seen from VS 18's Microsoft.NuGet.targets during solution
      restore/build.)

      This project has no .NET code and no TargetFramework at all — it's a pure TypeScript/npm
      VS Code extension built via the JS SDK's own BuildCommand (npm run build), not MSBuild
      compilation. Some NuGet/VS versions don't recognize Microsoft.VisualStudio.JavaScript.Sdk
      as a non-package-reference project style by default, so NuGet's restore falls back to
      assuming a PackageReference-style .NET project and then can't find a TargetFramework to
      restore against. Setting RestoreProjectStyle explicitly to Unknown tells NuGet's restore
      target to skip package-graph restore for this project entirely, matching how the JS SDK
      actually builds it (via npm/tsc, not `dotnet restore`/PackageReference).
    -->
    <RestoreProjectStyle>Unknown</RestoreProjectStyle>
    <!--
      BuildCommand/WatchCommand/CleanCommand/StartupCommand run via MSBuild's Exec task
      (Microsoft.VisualStudio.JavaScript.Sdk's CoreCompile target). In some build contexts
      (observed: automation/remote sessions where ComSpec isn't set at Machine/User scope,
      only transiently on the invoking process) Exec's CreateProcess-based invocation can't
      resolve even bare "cmd" or "npm" via PATH/PATHEXT — MSB3073 exit code 9009. Not
      reproducible from an interactive prompt, where cmd/npm both resolve fine; this is an
      environment-inheritance gap in the specific process tree MSBuild's Exec task runs in.
      Fixed by invoking the Node.js install's known absolute path directly, bypassing PATH/
      ComSpec resolution entirely — matches this repo's Node.js install location
      (C:\Program Files\nodejs). If Node.js is ever installed elsewhere, override NodeJsDir
      via `-p:NodeJsDir=...` or edit below.
    -->
    <NodeJsDir Condition="'$(NodeJsDir)' == ''">C:\Program Files\nodejs</NodeJsDir>
    <!--
      Same PATH-resolution gap as NodeJsDir above, but for pwsh: bare "pwsh" fails with
      MSB3073 exit code 9009 from this Exec task even when `pwsh` resolves fine from an
      interactive prompt and even after PowerShell 7 is confirmed present in the Machine PATH
      registry value. The process tree MSBuild's Exec task runs in inherits a stale/cached
      environment block from before PowerShell 7 was installed; only a full path bypasses that
      resolution entirely. Matches this repo's PowerShell 7 install location
      (C:\Program Files\PowerShell\7). If installed elsewhere, override via
      `-p:PwshExe=...\pwsh.exe` or edit below.
    -->
    <PwshExe Condition="'$(PwshExe)' == ''">C:\Program Files\PowerShell\7\pwsh.exe</PwshExe>
    <!--
      BuildCommand runs the full VSIX packaging pipeline (same as PublishCommand below), not
      just `npm run build`/tsc, so a plain VS "Build Solution"/"Build All"/Rebuild produces
      build/dist/tokensaver.vsix (both MSIs bundled, no raw exes) without a separate manual
      step. It calls build-vsix.ps1, which does its own nested `dotnet publish` of
      TokenSaver.MCP and DraftModel.Loader plus two WiX MSI builds.

      IMPORTANT — this only works because TokenSaverMCP.slnx gives this project explicit
      <BuildDependency> entries on TokenSaver/TokenSaver.MCP/TokenSaver.MCP.csproj and
      DraftModel/DraftModel.Loader/DraftModel.Loader.csproj (see the .slnx's
      TokenSaver.VsCode.Extension.esproj entry). Without that, VS builds every project in the
      solution in parallel by default, and build-vsix.ps1's nested `dotnet publish` of
      TokenSaver.MCP (which itself depends on TokenSaver.Docs) would run CONCURRENTLY with
      VS's own solution-wide build compiling TokenSaver.Docs as one of its normal projects —
      two independent MSBuild invocations racing to write
      TokenSaver.Docs\obj\Debug\net10.0\...GeneratedMSBuildEditorConfig.editorconfig at the
      same time. That was hit for real here: MSB3073 from Sdk.targets plus "The process cannot
      access the file ... because it is being used by another process." as the causal error,
      and it isn't safe to just retry past — it's inherent to two builds of the same project
      overlapping. The BuildDependency entries force VS to fully finish building
      TokenSaver.MCP.csproj/DraftModel.Loader.csproj (and everything they depend on,
      including TokenSaver.Docs) before this project's build starts, so by the time
      build-vsix.ps1's own `dotnet publish` runs, nothing else in the solution build is still
      touching those projects' obj/ output. If you ever see the
      GeneratedMSBuildEditorConfig.editorconfig file-lock error again, check the
      BuildDependency entries in TokenSaverMCP.slnx before assuming this script is broken —
      that's almost certainly what regressed.

      Always passes -NoInstall: a Build/Rebuild shouldn't reach out and uninstall/reinstall
      the developer's local VS Code extension as a side effect the way the script does by
      default when run manually from a terminal. Use scripts/build-vsix.ps1 directly
      (without -NoInstall) for that, or use the "Publish" action / `dotnet publish` on this
      .esproj, which runs the identical pipeline (kept in sync with BuildCommand below).

      -NoBuild lets `dotnet build|publish -p:SkipDotnetPublish=true` skip the (slow) nested
      dotnet publish of TokenSaver.MCP/DraftModel.Loader and the WiX MSI rebuilds, and just
      repackage the VSIX from whatever is already in bin/ — useful for iterating on the
      TypeScript/package.json side only without waiting on two MSI builds every time.
    -->
    <BuildCommand>"$(PwshExe)" -NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File "$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..\..\..\..\scripts\build-vsix.ps1" -NoInstall$(_BuildVsixNoBuildArg)</BuildCommand>
    <WatchCommand>"$(NodeJsDir)\npm.cmd" run watch</WatchCommand>
    <CleanCommand>"$(NodeJsDir)\npx.cmd" rimraf dist</CleanCommand>
    <StartupCommand>"$(NodeJsDir)\npm.cmd" run watch</StartupCommand>
    <!-- Publish runs the identical pipeline as BuildCommand above — see that property's
         comment for the full rationale and the BuildDependency requirement in
         TokenSaverMCP.slnx. Kept as a separate, equivalent property since some workflows
         (CI, `dotnet publish` from the CLI) invoke Publish explicitly rather than Build. -->
    <PublishCommand>"$(PwshExe)" -NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File "$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..\..\..\..\scripts\build-vsix.ps1" -NoInstall$(_BuildVsixNoBuildArg)</PublishCommand>
  </PropertyGroup>
  <PropertyGroup Condition="'$(SkipDotnetPublish)' == 'true'">
    <_BuildVsixNoBuildArg> -NoBuild</_BuildVsixNoBuildArg>
  </PropertyGroup>
</Project>